#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193719} :activity/id #uuid "b6520ec4-4c2b-5d03-b5d7-fdb5074438f4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048898 :topic/id #uuid "ca45e315-1972-57c0-8e15-999d27a51d68" :topic/math-academy-id 3944} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193719 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193826 :tutorial/content "In this lesson, we'll learn how to compute inverses of reciprocal functions.\n\nAs an example, let's consider the function $f(x)$, given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse $f^{-1}(x)$, we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = \\dfrac{7}{2x+4}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{7}{2y+4}\n$$\n\nThen, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{7}{2y + 4} \\\\\nx(2y + 4) &= 7 \\\\\n2xy + 4x &= 7 \\\\\n2xy &= 7 - 4x \\\\\ny &= \\frac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\dfrac{7 - 4x}{2x}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "1be3fc12-b770-5a76-ab56-1385a320d85f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6512 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c89b0360-91da-5424-aedb-da76a815447b" :step/math-academy-id 466026 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193720}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193720 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192814 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192405 :question/id #uuid "0042ce26-8793-5690-b4ab-9f018afe979a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10459"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "421b9396-2a7b-5c45-897a-9de5f52683de" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049461} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049636} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050383} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050908}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275199}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Inverse of a Reciprocal Fun